// wdoc section: Formatting (text & inline patterns, headings, code).

// Accent for the custom `deploy` callout in the "Callouts" example below.
// A class's `accent` field sets a callout's accent colour (heading, border,
// icon) — no CSS needed.
class "deploy" { accent = "#b48ead" }

page wdoc_formatting { sites = [:reference]  title = "Formatting"
  h1 "Formatting"

  p "The everyday building blocks of a page: prose paragraphs with inline formatting, the six heading levels, and syntax-highlighted code listings."

  h2 "Paragraphs with p"

  p "`p \"...\"` is the canonical paragraph shorthand. The label text is the paragraph's content, and inline patterns light up automatically. Previewed in a card:"

  diagram { width = 460  height = 110
    card { x = 16.0  y = 12.0  width = 428.0  height = 86.0
      p "Quick prose with **bold** and `inline code`."
      p "A whole paragraph in one line."
    }
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
p "Quick prose with **bold** and `inline code`."
p "A whole paragraph in one line."
WCL
  }

  p "Its fields (generated from the schema with `type_table`):"

  type_table { type = P }

  p "For a paragraph mixing several styled segments, use a `text` block holding `span`s — each `span` takes its own `class`."

  h2 "Inline patterns"

  p "The following patterns are recognised in any `p` body, in spans inside a `text` block, and in `utf8` table cells:"
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
**bold**             // → bold class
_italic_             // → italic class
`code`               // → monospace inline code
[text](page)         // → in-site link to a page
[text](site:page)    // → cross-site link
[text](https://...)  // → external link
:lucide.check:       // → inline icon (pack.name)
$x^2$                // → inline LaTeX (text style)
$$\int x \, dx$$     // → display-style LaTeX
WCL
  }

  p "Live, all in one paragraph:"

  diagram { width = 460  height = 90
    card { x = 16.0  y = 12.0  width = 428.0  height = 66.0
      p "Here is **bold**, _italic_, `code`, a [link](wdoc_overview), an icon :lucide.check:, and math $x^2$."
    }
  }

  h2 "Headings"

  p "Six heading levels are available — `h1` through `h6` — each taking the heading text as a single inline label and an optional `id`. Previewed in a card (kept out of this page's own outline):"

  diagram { width = 460  height = 280
    card { x = 16.0  y = 12.0  width = 428.0  height = 270.0
      h1 "Heading 1"
      h2 "Heading 2"
      h3 "Heading 3"
      h4 "Heading 4"
      h5 "Heading 5"
      h6 "Heading 6"
    }
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
h1 "Title"             { id = top }
h2 "Section"
h3 "Subsection"
h4 "Details"
h5 "Fine points"
h6 "Minutiae"
WCL
  }

  p "Each `h1`–`h6` takes the heading text as its inline label plus an optional `id` (the link target for cross-page anchors). No other fields."

  h2 "Code blocks"

  p "`code <lang> { source = ... }` renders a syntax-highlighted listing. The language tag picks the grammar; an unknown tag falls back to plain text. A live, highlighted listing:"

  diagram { width = 460  height = 214
    card { x = 16.0  y = 12.0  width = 428.0  height = 190.0
      code rust {
        source = <<'RUST'
fn fib(n: u32) -> u64 {
    match n {
        0 => 0,
        1 => 1,
        _ => fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2),
    }
}
RUST
      }
    }
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
code rust {
  source = <<'RUST'
fn fib(n: u32) -> u64 {
    match n {
        0 => 0,
        1 => 1,
        _ => fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2),
    }
}
RUST
}
WCL
  }

  p "Its fields:"

  type_table { type = Code }

  p "wdoc highlights a wide range of languages (via syntect + two-face). The tag is the language name or a common file extension; WCL ships its own grammar, so `code wcl { … }` highlights too. Supported tags include:"

  column {
    widths = [33.3, 33.3, 33.3]
    list {
      li "`rust`"
      li "`python`"
      li "`javascript`"
      li "`typescript`"
      li "`go`"
      li "`c`"
      li "`cpp`"
      li "`java`"
      li "`kotlin`"
    }
    list {
      li "`ruby`"
      li "`php`"
      li "`swift`"
      li "`html`"
      li "`css`"
      li "`scss`"
      li "`json`"
      li "`yaml`"
      li "`toml`"
    }
    list {
      li "`xml`"
      li "`markdown`"
      li "`sql`"
      li "`bash`"
      li "`dockerfile`"
      li "`lua`"
      li "`haskell`"
      li "`scala`"
      li "`wcl`"
    }
  }

  p "The `source` is usually a heredoc — use a raw heredoc (`<<'TAG'`) so the body is taken verbatim, with no escapes or interpolation to mangle backslash-heavy code. See Strings & Heredocs for the full syntax."

  h2 "Lists"

  p "A `list` holds `li` items. It's a bullet list by default; set `style = :numbered` for a numbered one. Each `li`'s text runs through the inline-pattern engine, so **bold**, `code`, links, and icons all work inside items. A live bullet and numbered list:"

  diagram { width = 460  height = 150
    card { x = 16.0  y = 12.0  width = 428.0  height = 126.0
      column {
        widths = [50.0, 50.0]
        list {
          li "Plain item"
          li "With **bold** and a [link](wdoc_overview)"
        }
        list { style = :numbered
          li "First step"
          li "Second step"
        }
      }
    }
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
list {
  li "Plain item"
  li "With **bold** and a [link](wdoc_overview)"
}

list { style = :numbered
  li "First step"
  li "Second step"
}
WCL
  }

  p "Nest an `li` inside an `li` for a sublist — it inherits the parent list's style, so a numbered list numbers sublists hierarchically (`2.1`, `2.2`). For a sublist with a *different* style, drop a whole `list` block inside the `li` instead. Live:"

  diagram { width = 460  height = 200
    card { x = 16.0  y = 12.0  width = 428.0  height = 176.0
      list { style = :numbered
        li "Setup"
        li "Build" {
          li "Compile"
          li "Link"
        }
        li "Run" {
          list {
            li "Foreground"
            li "Background"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
list { style = :numbered
  li "Setup"
  li "Build" {
    li "Compile"          // renders 2.1
    li "Link"             // renders 2.2
  }
  li "Run" {
    list {                // a bulleted sublist inside a numbered list
      li "Foreground"
      li "Background"
    }
  }
}
WCL
  }

  p "The `list` block's fields:"

  type_table { type = ListBlock }

  p "And each `li`:"

  type_table { type = Li }

  h2 "Callouts"

  p "A `callout` is an admonition: an icon, a coloured heading, and a body. Six built-in types are selected by `class`, each shipping a default colour and icon. Live — all six, plus a custom `deploy` type at the end:"

  diagram { width = 460  height = 560
    card { x = 16.0  y = 12.0  width = 428.0  height = 536.0
      callout "Note"     { class = ["note"]     body = "Background context the reader should remember." }
      callout "Info"     { class = ["info"]     body = "Neutral information worth surfacing." }
      callout "Tip"      { class = ["tip"]      body = "A helpful shortcut or best practice." }
      callout "Warning"  { class = ["warning"]  body = "Something to be careful about." }
      callout "Error"    { class = ["error"]    body = "A failure or hard constraint." }
      callout "Success"  { class = ["success"]  body = "Confirm an action completed." }
      callout "Deploying" {
        class = ["deploy"]
        icon  = "lucide.rocket"
        body  = "A **custom** type — a class sets its accent and `icon` picks the glyph."
      }
    }
  }

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
callout "Note"    { class = ["note"]    body = "Background context the reader should remember." }
callout "Info"    { class = ["info"]    body = "Neutral information worth surfacing." }
callout "Tip"     { class = ["tip"]     body = "A helpful shortcut or best practice." }
callout "Warning" { class = ["warning"] body = "Something to be careful about." }
callout "Error"   { class = ["error"]   body = "A failure or hard constraint." }
callout "Success" { class = ["success"] body = "Confirm an action completed." }

// A custom type: a class's `accent` field supplies the colour, `icon` the glyph.
class "deploy" { accent = "#b48ead" }
callout "Deploying" {
  class = ["deploy"]
  icon  = "lucide.rocket"
  body  = "A **custom** type — the class above sets its accent colour."
}
WCL
  }

  p "The six built-in types ship their own accent. For a custom type, give a `class` an `accent` colour and list it in the callout's `class` — that sets the accent (heading, border, icon) with no CSS. The `icon` field picks any glyph. See [Styling](wdoc_styling) for the full class reference."

  p "Its fields:"

  type_table { type = Callout }
}
